\documentclass{article}
\title{Feeder Aggregation Error in Distribution Grid State Estimation}
\author{J. Baranski}
\date{February 2026}
\begin{document}
\maketitle
\section{Introduction}
Distribution grids increasingly rely on aggregated load models
because individual smart meter streams are too noisy to use directly.
We quantify how the estimation error grows with the depth of aggregation.

\section{Error Growth}
Under mild independence assumptions the mismatch scales like \sqrt{2}$
each time the aggregation depth doubles,
so shallow aggregation trees are strongly preferred.
This scaling is confirmed on three European feeders with real metering.

\section{Feeder Characteristics}
The three study feeders differ in length and customer mix.
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\hline
Feeder & Length (km) \\
\hline
Feeder A & 3.4 \\
Feeder B & 1.9 & 0.8 \\
Feeder C & 5.2 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
Feeder C is the longest and shows the largest estimation error at depth four.

\section{Mitigation}
Placing a small number of additional sensors at intermediate nodes
halves the error growth rate in all three feeders.
The optimal placement follows the betweenness centrality of the feeder graph.

\section{Conclusion}
Aggregation depth is a first-order design parameter for state estimation,
and a handful of well placed sensors compensates most of the penalty.
\end{document}

